    "title": "Michigan vs. UConn: Who is the National Championship favorite? (LIve odds)",
    "text": "It's going to be a heavyweight championship bout for the ages on Monday night in Indianapolis at Lucas Oil Stadium between the UConn Huskies and the Michigan Wolverines.Both men's college basketball teams have fought through every other team in their way to make it to the national title game.For the Huskies, it's all about legacy. Dan Hurley can take his legend to even a greater stature by winning his third national title in four seasons and solidifying UConn as a true dynasty within college sports.On the other end, for the top-seeded Wolverines, it's about regaining what they haven't held in over three decades. Before the \"Fab Five\" took over college basketball, the 1989 Wolverines were the last team to hoist a national title for one of the most heralded collegiate sports programs in the country.It's the mythic aura that surrounds the Huskies following another impossible run to the final, with their iconic comeback against Duke lingering in people's minds against the outright force of the Wolverines, who began the year blowing out teams by 30 points and haven't looked back since.Who is the favorite?More news: What UConn’s Dan Hurley Did Immediately After Final Four Win Was WeirdUConn Huskies head coach Dan Hurley reacts during the first half in the second round of the 2026 NCAA Men's Basketball Tournament at Xfinity Mobile Ar... | (Photo by Emilee Chinn/Getty Images)More news: NCAA Facing Backlash for Awful Final Four Seating — See the Worst ViewsPer Kalshi, the prediction website, UConn's mystique isn't enough to convince people they can beat Michigan's dominance.73% of predictors on Kalshi currently believe Michigan will lift the national championship by the end of Monday in Indianapolis.For UConn, being an underdog this tournament, even as a No. 2 seed, isn't anything new. On Kalshi, one week ago, during their Duke game, they were below 1% before reeling off one of the most memorable comebacks in college basketball history to win the game on a Braylon Mullins three-pointer.",
